ghosts:
  - id: "GHOST-001"
    tier: "warm"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-23"
    titre: "Archive before dashboard"
    l0_abstract: "Implement `scribe archive` before `scribe dashboard` so memory lifecycle rules exist before visualization."
    l2_details: >
      Dashboard is useful for observability, but it should consume stable lifecycle semantics rather than invent them.
      Archive is the lower-level primitive: it can move cold entries out of the hot operational path, preserve causality,
      and keep future dashboard views smaller, faster, and less misleading.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "scribe dashboard first"
        raison_rejet: "A dashboard before archive would visualize a growing YAML without solving retention, cold storage, or signal/noise pressure."
    evidence:
      type: "REASONED"
      source: "JOURNAL-025"
      observable: "`compact/promote/export` are validated; the next missing lifecycle primitive is archival, not presentation."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-025"
      renforce: ["PAT-007", "PAT-008", "PAT-009"]
  - id: "GHOST-002"
    tier: "hot"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-24"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-24"
    titre: "Lock multi-agent local choisi"
    l0_abstract: "SCRIBE mutations use one local JSON lock in `scribe-out/locks/scribe.lock` before adding database or server coordination."
    l2_details: >
      The immediate risk is concurrent local agent writes to the same YAML memory. A tiny lock file with agent, session,
      pid, timestamp, surface, and TTL prevents accidental overlap while staying portable with the rootless bundle.
      Doctor and read-only commands stay unblocked so diagnosis remains possible during lock ownership.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "Pas de lock"
        raison_rejet: "Corruption possible si deux agents modifient le SCRIBE en parallèle."
      - nom: "Lock base de données"
        raison_rejet: "Sur-ingénierie pour un bundle local portable et copiable entre projets."
    ne_pas_reproposer: ["Pas de lock", "Lock base de données"]
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-045"
      observable: "`scribe lock acquire/status/release` fonctionne et les commandes mutantes refusent sans verrou actif."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-045"
      renforce: ["SCAR-001", "PAT-009", "PAT-020"]
  - id: "GHOST-003"
    tier: "hot"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-24"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-24"
    titre: "state.json partagé pour resynchronisation multi-agent"
    l0_abstract: "A shared `scribe-out/state.json` records the last real SCRIBE writer and hash so agents can detect stale context before acting."
    l2_details: >
      The lock prevents concurrent writes, but it does not tell a second agent that its context is stale after another agent has already written.
      The chosen state file stores a hash, line count, writer object, last session, changed IDs, and closed write_kind enum. `sync` reads and alerts
      without becoming the last writer; `sync --repair` atomically rebuilds state after legitimate manual recovery.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "HEAD.json"
        raison_rejet: "The Git analogy implies branch/version semantics the SCRIBE state file does not provide."
      - nom: "sync met à jour last_writer"
        raison_rejet: "A reader is not an écrivain; overwriting last_writer hides the true source of the latest memory mutation."
      - nom: "last_entry_id seul"
        raison_rejet: "Tier/status/archive mutations can change SCRIBE state without appending a new entity."
    ne_pas_reproposer: ["HEAD.json", "sync met à jour last_writer", "last_entry_id seul"]
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-046"
      observable: "`scribe sync` reports stale/missing state, `sync --repair` writes state.json atomically, and doctor warns W011/W012/W013."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-046"
      renforce: ["GHOST-002", "PAT-020", "PAT-003"]
  - id: "GHOST-004"
    tier: "warm"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-25"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-25"
    titre: "Migration Clean Architecture progressive du frontend"
    l0_abstract: "The JJK frontend now uses one canonical Clean Architecture surface: `src/app`, `src/core`, and `src/shared`; legacy top-level adapters were removed after imports were migrated."
    l2_details: >
      The clean architecture pack was first applied as a progressive migration, then tightened after the user caught duplicate top-level folders.
      `src/app` delegates to presentation components, business validation/use cases live under `core/domain` and `core/application`, Redux/RTK Query/realtime details live under `core/infrastructure`, and cross-layer utilities live under `src/shared`. The temporary top-level `src/components`, `src/store`, and `src/utils` adapters are now removed to keep the frontend DRY and unambiguous.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "Big-bang deletion of old frontend paths during the first migration pass"
        raison_rejet: "Too risky in a dirty worktree before import convergence; later removal became safe after graph/import scans and validation showed the adapters were unused."
      - nom: "Introduce Next proxy during this refactor"
        raison_rejet: "The backend contract currently exposes direct REST plus WebSocket send; adding a proxy would be a behavior and deployment change, not only a clean-architecture restructure."
    ne_pas_reproposer: ["Big-bang deletion of old frontend paths", "Silent Next proxy switch"]
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-057"
      observable: "`npm run lint`, `npx tsc --noEmit`, `npm run build`, import scans, `curl -I /login`, and Graphify rebuild passed after migration and after removing unused legacy adapters."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-057"
      renforce: ["PAT-024", "INV-F003", "JOURNAL-060"]
  - id: "GHOST-005"
    tier: "warm"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-25"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-25"
    titre: "Cookie-backed auth boundary choisi"
    l0_abstract: "Auth now treats browser JavaScript as untrusted: access and refresh secrets live in HttpOnly cookies, while the frontend only stores session identity."
    l2_details: >
      The hardening repays the browser-stored JWT debt without inserting a Next.js proxy layer. Direct backend cookies keep the existing REST and Socket.IO topology, refresh rotation narrows stolen-refresh-token lifetime, and a readable CSRF cookie plus origin policy protects unsafe browser requests without exposing bearer tokens to Redux or localStorage. JOURNAL-071 closes the remaining ambiguity by removing the REST Authorization Bearer fallback; authenticated REST and Socket.IO now share the same cookie authority unless a future explicit API-product GHOST reopens bearer auth.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "Keep JWT in localStorage for simplicity"
        raison_rejet: "A single XSS would expose the bearer token and keep the original DEBT-003 production gap alive."
      - nom: "Move auth through a Next.js proxy in this step"
        raison_rejet: "The current deployment already has direct REST plus Socket.IO; adding a proxy would widen the behavior and ops change beyond the debt repayment."
      - nom: "Keep REST Authorization Bearer fallback after cookie auth"
        raison_rejet: "Two simultaneous auth authorities make the security boundary ambiguous; bearer API support must be an explicit product decision, not leftover compatibility."
    ne_pas_reproposer: ["Browser-stored bearer JWT", "Silent Next proxy switch during auth debt repayment", "REST Bearer fallback without explicit API product decision"]
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-058"
      observable: "Backend and frontend builds pass after HttpOnly cookie sessions, refresh rotation, CSRF header validation, origin checks, and removal of Redux/localStorage token persistence."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-058"
      rembourse: ["DEBT-003"]
      renforce: ["GHOST-004", "VAC-003", "INV-F003"]
  - id: "GHOST-006"
    tier: "warm"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-26"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-26"
    titre: "SCRIBE-RAG read-only au-dessus de SEL"
    l0_abstract: "SCRIBE-RAG V1 reads canonical SEL JSON export, builds a portable BM25 cache, and does not replace SEL ownership of writes, doctor, lock, sync, or state."
    l2_details: >
      Phase 0 measured the existing retrieval at 2/4 on semantic safety cases: localStorage and client-side token storage were the gaps, while refresh-race and HttpOnly cookie auth were reachable.
      V1 therefore builds a separate read-only layer under .agent/workflow/scribe-rag, resolves the SEL CLI from a canonical absolute path relative to its own file, writes only generated cache under scribe-out/rag-index.json, and keeps embeddings optional until a post-scoring eval proves they are needed.
      Phase 8 passed 8/8 with BM25-only scoring, so sentence-transformers remains skipped for portability.
      After review on 2026-05-26, root AGENTS.md marks scribe-rag as experimental optional preflight only; it must not become mandatory until five real jjk-messenger implementation/debug/refactor sessions document 0 retrieval regressions.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "Import direct de scripts internes SEL"
        raison_rejet: "Private Python imports would couple scribe-rag to SEL internals and create silent breakage on refactor."
      - nom: "PATH scribe non canonique"
        raison_rejet: "A PATH lookup can hit a legacy root adapter or global command and recreate split-brain behavior across machines."
      - nom: "sentence-transformers obligatoire sans mesure"
        raison_rejet: "A heavy runtime dependency would weaken portability when BM25 plus negative-memory scoring already passes the current eval."
      - nom: "Archiver ou fusionner SEL après RAG V1"
        raison_rejet: "SEL remains the write and guard authority; scribe-rag is a read/retrieval layer, not a replacement."
    ne_pas_reproposer: ["Import direct de scripts internes SEL", "PATH scribe non canonique", "sentence-transformers obligatoire sans mesure", "Archiver SEL après RAG V1"]
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-073"
      observable: "scribe-rag eval --force passed 8/8 in BM25 mode; five synthetic drills passed, but root AGENTS.md now keeps scribe-rag experimental until five real sessions prove it."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-073"
      renforce: ["PAT-007", "PAT-018", "PAT-023", "PAT-024", "GHOST-002", "GHOST-003"]
  - id: "GHOST-007"
    tier: "hot"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-26"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-26"
    titre: "SCRIBE-RAG BM25 canonical preflight choisi"
    l0_abstract: "scribe-rag BM25 becomes the canonical portable read-only preflight; SEL remains the canonical write/doctor/lock/state substrate and Hybrid remains optional."
    l2_details: >
      The benchmark favored scribe-rag BM25 over SEL and Hybrid for the default portable preflight.
      Exact output measurements were: SEL context 18 lines/170 words/0.096308s, SEL query 34 lines/221 words/0.396621s;
      BM25 context 15 lines/155 words/0.130306s, BM25 query 12 lines/125 words/0.126069s;
      Hybrid context 15 lines/154 words/17.628567s, Hybrid query 12 lines/131 words/21.760626s.
      Retrieval top-3 correctness was SEL 2/4, BM25 4/4, Hybrid 4/4. Top-1 correctness was SEL 0/4, BM25 2/4, Hybrid 3/4.
      Challenge reliability: localStorage JWT was SEL WARN, BM25 STOP, Hybrid STOP; cookies HttpOnly refresh rotation was SEL BLOCK, BM25 REVIEW, Hybrid REVIEW.
      Hybrid improved one top-1 semantic case but added sentence-transformers 5.5.1, a 168M all-MiniLM-L6-v2 cache, and 17-24s per context/query process.
      Therefore BM25 is the default canonical preflight; Hybrid is reserved for explicit deep semantic experiments, not project bootstrap.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "SEL comme preflight retrieval principal"
        raison_rejet: >
          SEL used more output for query, missed two semantic retrieval cases in top-3, and returned WARN instead of STOP for the localStorage JWT challenge.
      - nom: "RAG Hybrid obligatoire par defaut"
        raison_rejet: >
          Hybrid matched BM25 on top-3 correctness and challenge decisions but cost 17.628567s for context, 21.760626s for query, and required sentence-transformers plus a 168M model cache.
      - nom: "sentence-transformers obligatoire sur projet vide"
        raison_rejet: >
          Empty-project bootstrap plus Hybrid build measured 28.159107s with the model already cached, while BM25 bootstrap plus build measured 1.575815s.
    ne_pas_reproposer: ["SEL-only retrieval preflight", "Hybrid mandatory by default", "sentence-transformers mandatory for portable bootstrap"]
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-076"
      observable: "Benchmark artifact /tmp/scribe_rag_benchmark_2026-05-26.json plus /tmp/scribe_rag_bootstrap_2026-05-26.json recorded the measured outputs."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-076"
      renforce: ["GHOST-006", "PAT-024", "GHOST-003"]
  - id: "GHOST-008"
    tier: "hot"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-26"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-26"
    titre: "SEL rôle moteur interne uniquement"
    l0_abstract: "SEL est le moteur de garde et d'écriture; scribe-rag BM25 est la seule interface agent pour retrieval."
    l2_details: >
      SEL must stay intact because scribe-rag depends on its canonical CLI for export and because the project still needs SEL for bootstrap, doctor, lock, sync/state, archive, graph maintenance, and SCRIBE writes.
      The operational boundary is now explicit: agents use scribe-rag for build/context/query/explain/challenge, while SEL remains under the hood for guardrails and stateful memory operations.
      Hybrid remains an escalation path only when scribe-rag eval drops below 7/8; BM25 remains canonical while eval is green.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "Archivage complet SEL"
        raison_rejet: "scribe-rag appelle SEL via CLI. Archiver SEL = casser scribe-rag et perdre doctor/lock/state/export/bootstrap."
      - nom: "SEL comme interface directe agent"
        raison_rejet: "Trop verbeux: 34 lignes de query contre 12 pour scribe-rag BM25, et 2/4 retrieval sémantique sur le benchmark."
    ne_pas_reproposer:
      - "Appeler SEL directement depuis AGENTS.md"
      - "Archiver SEL tant que scribe-rag l'utilise"
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-078"
      observable: "AGENTS.md and the install template now route agent retrieval to scribe-rag while keeping SEL for bootstrap, doctor, lock, sync/state, export, archive, graph maintenance, and writes."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-078"
      renforce: ["GHOST-006", "GHOST-007", "PAT-027", "GHOST-003"]
  - id: "GHOST-009"
    tier: "hot"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-26"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-26"
    titre: "1 surface = 1 agent maximum"
    l0_abstract: "4 agents simultanés sans surface exclusive = catastrophe. Expérience prouvée."
    l2_details: >
      Le modèle multi-agent mature n'est pas un lock global autour de toute la codebase. Chaque agent reçoit une surface exclusive d'écriture applicative, peut lire les autres surfaces, puis utilise SEL lock seulement pour les écritures SCRIBE. L'orchestrateur reste responsable de l'attribution des surfaces et des merges Git.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "4 agents sur même codebase sans surfaces"
        raison_rejet: "Collision fichiers, corruption SCRIBE, agents perdus. Testé et échoué."
      - nom: "Lock global unique pour tout"
        raison_rejet: "Deadlock certain sur 4 agents. Surfaces exclusives suffisent."
    ne_pas_reproposer:
      - "4 agents sans surfaces exclusives"
      - "Lock global unique multi-agent"
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-079"
      observable: "La feuille V4 adoptée documente explicitement les surfaces exclusives après retour d'expérience multi-agent et rejette les agents sans surfaces."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-079"
      renforce: ["GHOST-002", "GHOST-003", "GHOST-008", "PAT-MA-001"]
  - id: "GHOST-010"
    tier: "hot"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-26"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-26"
    titre: "REST auth cookie-only, Bearer fallback absent"
    l0_abstract: "REST auth is currently cookie-only; Authorization Bearer must not be reintroduced without an explicit API product decision."
    l2_details: >
      The current authMiddleware reads only the HttpOnly access-token cookie and there is no Authorization/Bearer path in backend src, frontend src, or backend scripts.
      This preserves the browser-security boundary chosen in GHOST-005: browser JavaScript remains untrusted and secrets stay out of Redux, localStorage, and request headers.
      A future machine-to-machine API can still exist, but it must be a deliberate product/API decision with its own threat model, tests, rate limits, and documented contract, not a silent fallback in the browser auth middleware.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "Réintroduire Authorization Bearer dans authMiddleware"
        raison_rejet: "Créerait deux autorités d'auth REST et contournerait la frontière cookie-only validée pour le navigateur."
      - nom: "REST Bearer fallback without explicit API product decision"
        raison_rejet: "Un fallback non décidé réouvre une surface de vol de token et reste invisible aux tests produit."
      - nom: "Browser-stored bearer JWT"
        raison_rejet: "Déjà rejeté par GHOST-005: une XSS exposerait le secret de session."
    ne_pas_reproposer:
      - "Réintroduire Authorization Bearer dans authMiddleware"
      - "REST Bearer fallback without explicit API product decision"
      - "Browser-stored bearer JWT"
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-080"
      observable: "authMiddleware.ts reads ACCESS_TOKEN_COOKIE_NAME through getCookie, and rg found no Authorization/Bearer usage in backend src, frontend src, or backend scripts."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-080"
      renforce: ["GHOST-005", "PAT-027", "VAC-009"]
  - id: "GHOST-011"
    tier: "hot"
    status: "ACTIVE"
    date: "2026-05-26"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-26"
    titre: "Bundle SCRIBE portable mono-dossier"
    l0_abstract: "Le workflow portable canonique est `.agent/workflow/scribe/`; copier ce seul dossier suffit pour bootstrapper SEL et scribe-rag dans un nouveau projet."
    l2_details: >
      La portabilité réelle exige une identité de bundle unique, pas deux dossiers voisins à déplacer ensemble.
      Le root `.agent/workflow/scribe/` contient `sel/`, `rag/`, les wrappers racine `scribe` et `scribe-rag`, sans aucun dossier workflow sibling hérité visible.
      Les résolveurs RAG cherchent le root portable par structure `.agent/workflow/scribe`, SEL bootstrap installe ce root entier dans les projets hôtes, et les templates AGENTS/rules/CI pointent vers les commandes racine.
      Les anciens chemins ne sont plus fournis; les prompts, scripts ou agents qui les utilisent doivent être corrigés vers `.agent/workflow/scribe/` au lieu de maintenir des shims.
    alternatives_rejetees:
      - nom: "Copier SEL et scribe-rag comme deux dossiers séparés"
        raison_rejet: "Trop fragile pour les nouveaux projets: un agent peut oublier un dossier, casser la résolution SEL/RAG ou dériver les templates."
      - nom: "Garder les anciens dossiers comme shims visibles"
        raison_rejet: "Fonctionnellement compatible mais contradictoire avec la promesse mono-dossier: cela crée deux points d'entrée apparents et entretient la dette de chemins."
      - nom: "Laisser les templates pointer les anciens chemins"
        raison_rejet: "Bootstrap réintroduirait l'ancienne topologie et annulerait la portabilité mono-dossier."
    ne_pas_reproposer:
      - "Copier séparément `.agent/workflow/scribe-engineering-local-causal-retrieval/` et `.agent/workflow/scribe-rag/`"
      - "Faire de `.agent/workflow/scribe-rag/` une source canonique séparée"
      - "Recréer des dossiers de shims visibles pour les anciens chemins"
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-082"
      observable: "Les anciens dossiers `.agent/workflow/scribe-engineering-local-causal-retrieval/` et `.agent/workflow/scribe-rag/` ont été supprimés; la copie portable de `.agent/workflow/scribe/` bootstrappe et exécute RAG/doctor/whoami seule."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-082"
      renforce: ["GHOST-007", "GHOST-008", "GHOST-009", "VAC-006", "PAT-024"]
hypotheses: []
lessons: []
debts:
  - id: "DEBT-001"
    tier: "cold"
    status: "REMBOURSEE"
    titre: "Process-local auth rate limiter"
    date_creation: "2026-05-23"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-25"
    type: "CHOISIE"
    severite: "HIGH"
    l0_abstract: "The auth limiter is no longer process-local; auth attempts are counted atomically in PostgreSQL and shared across backend instances."
    l2_details: >
      The first hardening pass used an in-memory limiter to avoid adding infrastructure. JOURNAL-064 replaces that with `RateLimitBucket` and `PrismaRateLimitStore`, so login/register counters are keyed by scope and client identity in PostgreSQL. The atomic upsert prevents per-process counter divergence during horizontal scaling while keeping the route middleware before validation and controller execution.
    plan_remboursement: "REMBOURSEE: `RateLimitBucket` migration, `PrismaRateLimitStore`, and async auth rate-limit middleware now provide shared PostgreSQL enforcement."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-064"
      renforce: ["VAC-003", "INV-F003", "GHOST-005"]
    status_log:
      - date: "2026-05-25"
        from: "ACTIVE"
        to: "REMBOURSEE"
        reason: "Auth rate limiting moved from process-local Map state to PostgreSQL shared atomic buckets."
    tier_log:
      - date: "2026-05-25"
        from: "warm"
        to: "cold"
        reason: "Debt is repaid and kept searchable as historical auth governance."
  - id: "DEBT-002"
    tier: "cold"
    status: "REMBOURSEE"
    titre: "Smoke retrieval graphify dual ambiguity"
    date_creation: "2026-05-24"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-24"
    scope: "dev"
    type: "OBSERVED_RETRIEVAL_GAP"
    severite: "MEDIUM"
    l0_abstract: "`scribe eval --smoke` originally missed the graphify application/bundle confusion owner; PAT-024 now owns that distinction."
    evidence:
      type: "OBSERVED"
      source: "JOURNAL-050"
      observable: "`scribe eval --smoke` passed 4/4 after the smoke expectation moved from PAT-023 to dedicated PAT-024 and the bundle graph output path was renamed to `scribe-out/bundle-graph/`."
    l2_details: >
      The failure was a retrieval-quality gap, not a runtime crash. PAT-023 remains the cleanup/noise rule;
      PAT-024 now owns the distinct Graphify app-vs-bundle graph naming rule.
    plan_remboursement: "REMBOURSEE: PAT-024 hot plus `scribe-out/bundle-graph/` rename resolved the semantic owner and `scribe eval --smoke` reaches 4/4."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-049"
      contribue_a: ["PAT-024"]
    status_log:
      - date: "2026-05-24"
        from: "ACTIVE"
        to: "REMBOURSEE"
        reason: "PAT-024 and the bundle-graph rename gave the smoke query a dedicated causal owner."
    tier_log:
      - date: "2026-05-24"
        from: "warm"
        to: "cold"
        reason: "Debt is repaid and kept searchable as historical retrieval governance."
  - id: "DEBT-003"
    tier: "cold"
    status: "REMBOURSEE"
    titre: "Browser-stored JWT session"
    date_creation: "2026-05-25"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-25"
    type: "CHOISIE"
    severite: "HIGH"
    l0_abstract: "The frontend no longer persists bearer JWTs in JavaScript-readable storage; session secrets are backend-set cookies with refresh rotation and CSRF/origin checks."
    l2_details: >
      DEBT-003 is repaid by moving access and refresh credentials to HttpOnly backend cookies, rotating refresh tokens with family reuse revocation, storing only public session identity in Redux, cleaning legacy localStorage keys, and requiring CSRF plus origin validation for unsafe browser requests.
    plan_remboursement: "REMBOURSEE: backend cookie/refresh-token support, frontend cookie-backed session hydration, and removal of token reads from Redux-visible client state are implemented and validated in JOURNAL-058."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-058"
      explique_par: ["GHOST-005"]
      renforce: ["VAC-003", "INV-F003"]
    status_log:
      - date: "2026-05-25"
        from: "ACTIVE"
        to: "REMBOURSEE"
        reason: "HttpOnly cookie sessions, refresh rotation, CSRF/origin policy, and frontend token removal now replace browser-stored JWTs."
    tier_log:
      - date: "2026-05-25"
        from: "warm"
        to: "cold"
        reason: "Debt is repaid and retained as historical auth governance."
  - id: "DEBT-004"
    tier: "cold"
    status: "REMBOURSEE"
    titre: "Refresh token rotation race"
    date_creation: "2026-05-25"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-25"
    type: "OBSERVED_AUDIT_FINDING"
    severite: "HIGH"
    l0_abstract: "Concurrent refresh requests can no longer mint multiple valid successor refresh tokens."
    l2_details: >
      `RefreshSessionUseCase` reads a non-revoked refresh token, then `PrismaRefreshTokenRepository.rotate` creates the next
      token before updating the current token by id only. Two concurrent refreshes can both read the old token as active,
      create two successor tokens in the same family, and then both mark the old token rotated. This weakens refresh-token
      reuse detection because the family can branch without a clear replay signal.
    plan_remboursement: "REMBOURSEE: `PrismaRefreshTokenRepository.rotate` now conditionally revokes the current token where `revokedAt` is null before creating the successor, `RefreshSessionUseCase` maps conflicts to reuse-detected family revocation plus 401, and `npm run test:refresh-rotation` verifies the single-winner path."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-061"
      explique_par: ["GHOST-005"]
      renforce: ["SCAR-003", "VAC-009", "VAC-003", "INV-F003"]
    status_log:
      - date: "2026-05-25"
        from: "ACTIVE"
        to: "REMBOURSEE"
        reason: "Refresh rotation became single-winner and conflict maps to reuse-detected family revocation."
    tier_log:
      - date: "2026-05-25"
        from: "warm"
        to: "cold"
        reason: "Debt is repaid and retained as historical auth governance."
  - id: "DEBT-005"
    tier: "cold"
    status: "REMBOURSEE"
    titre: "WebSocket session pressure boundary"
    date_creation: "2026-05-25"
    schema_patch_date: "2026-05-25"
    type: "OBSERVED_AUDIT_FINDING"
    severite: "HIGH"
    l0_abstract: "Socket.IO no longer accepts client-supplied auth-token fallback and now enforces connection and message pressure limits."
    l2_details: >
      `WebSocketService` now authenticates browser realtime sessions from the HttpOnly access cookie only. It rejects missing or invalid cookie auth, caps active sockets by user and remote address, and throttles `sendMessage` events per authenticated user window. The frontend already connects with credentials, so removing the JavaScript-provided handshake token path preserves the cookie-backed auth boundary.
    plan_remboursement: "REMBOURSEE: WebSocket auth is cookie-only and the service enforces per-user sockets, per-IP sockets, and per-user message windows via configurable `WS_*` limits."
    liens_causaux:
      source: "JOURNAL-064"
      explique_par: ["GHOST-005"]
      renforce: ["VAC-002", "INV-F003"]
    status_log:
      - date: "2026-05-25"
        from: "ACTIVE"
        to: "REMBOURSEE"
        reason: "Removed Socket.IO bearer/handshake fallback and added explicit connection/message pressure limits."
    tier_log:
      - date: "2026-05-25"
        from: "warm"
        to: "cold"
        reason: "Debt is repaid and retained as historical realtime governance."
mutations: []
journal:
  - id: "JOURNAL-001"
    date: "2026-05-20"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    l0_abstract: "Initialized TENOR session memory after confirming the project SCRIBE and template were absent."
    pourquoi: "The user requested TENOR initialization and last_update synchronization; creating a minimal YAML memory prevents future sessions from starting without causal memory."
  - id: "JOURNAL-002"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    l0_abstract: "Updated framework dependencies to current stable versions and repaired the lint/build path exposed by Next.js 16."
    pourquoi: "The dependency upgrade was not just a version bump: Next.js 16 removes `next lint`, so keeping the old lint command would make the project look upgraded while leaving validation broken."
  - id: "JOURNAL-003"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    l0_abstract: "Installed Graphify for Codex and generated the canonical root knowledge graph."
    pourquoi: "The project needed persistent structural memory aligned with Codex hooks; keeping the graph at root prevents future agents from missing it or reading a stale subfolder graph."
  - id: "JOURNAL-004"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    l0_abstract: "Implemented a read-only scribe doctor CLI with a Markdown report output."
    pourquoi: "The SCRIBE now needs mechanical validation before V3.2 migration work; keeping doctor read-only prevents tooling from silently rewriting historical memory."
  - id: "JOURNAL-005"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    l0_abstract: "Validated the existing SCRIBE with doctor: zero blocking ERRORs, five legacy WARNINGs intentionally kept."
    pourquoi: "The V3.2 roadmap forbids retroactive cleanup of old warnings during migration; documenting W001/W002/W009 preserves historical integrity while proving the SCRIBE is structurally safe."
    migration_warnings:
      errors_blocking: 0
      warnings_total: 5
      kept_as_legacy:
        - "W001: PAT-001 and VAC-001 pre-V3.2 missing scope."
        - "W002: PAT-001 and VAC-001 pre-V3.2 missing evidence.type."
        - "W009: legacy entries pre-V3.2 missing schema_patch_date."
  - id: "JOURNAL-006"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "QUICK"
    l0_abstract: "Engraved the full seven-step operational loop into the V3.2 scribe workflow template."
    pourquoi: "The prior wording could make agents treat scribe doctor as the last memory step; the template now states explicitly that every real task must write the causal SCRIBE delta before the final doctor check."
  - id: "JOURNAL-007"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["VAC-001", "PAT-001"]
    l0_abstract: "Hardened Socket.IO sendMessage ingestion with server-side validation and PII-safe logging."
    pourquoi: "WebSocket events bypass Express/Zod middleware, so the same production input-trust risk exists unless the socket boundary validates unknown payloads itself; logging only metadata prevents chat content exposure in debug logs."
    scribe_delta: "VAC-002"
  - id: "JOURNAL-008"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["PAT-001", "VAC-002"]
    l0_abstract: "Separated Graphify app scope from SCRIBE/TENOR tooling with .graphifyignore and an APP_SCOPE query convention."
    pourquoi: "The canonical root graph must guide application architecture; after tooling nodes polluted god-node rankings, excluding process tooling restored app-first navigation without deleting the tooling itself."
    scribe_delta: "PAT-002"
  - id: "JOURNAL-009"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["VAC-002", "PAT-002"]
    l0_abstract: "Added request throttling to public auth attempts before validation and controller execution."
    pourquoi: "Login and registration are public abuse targets; limiting attempts at the route boundary reduces brute-force pressure while keeping validation and business logic unchanged."
    scribe_delta: "VAC-003 | DEBT-001"
  - id: "JOURNAL-010"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["PAT-002", "VAC-003"]
    l0_abstract: "Validated scribe doctor with a controlled corruption crash-test on an isolated copy."
    pourquoi: "The memory validator must fail closed before it can be trusted; injecting duplicate IDs, missing V3.2 fields, broken links, and an unlogged status mutation proved doctor catches structural corruption while the canonical SCRIBE remains clean."
    scribe_delta: "JOURNAL-010"
  - id: "JOURNAL-011"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["PAT-002", "VAC-003", "PAT-003"]
    l0_abstract: "Promoted scribe doctor into a mandatory pre/post guard for every SCRIBE evolution."
    pourquoi: "After the controlled corruption test, doctor is no longer optional process hygiene; it is the mechanical gate that proves a memory delta starts from a sane state and does not corrupt the canonical SCRIBE."
    scribe_delta: "PAT-003 | JOURNAL-011"
  - id: "JOURNAL-012"
    date: "2026-05-23"
    mode: "STANDARD"
