  - id: "JOURNAL-045"
    date: "2026-05-24"
    mode: "CRITICAL"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["SCAR-001", "SCAR-002", "GHOST-002", "VAC-004", "VAC-005", "VAC-006", "PAT-003", "PAT-007", "PAT-012", "PAT-017", "PAT-019", "PAT-021"]
    l0_abstract: "Stabilized SCRIBE Phase 1 with a local mutation lock, typed edge counters, PAT audit, and hot-tier reduction."
    pourquoi: "The Claude critique was correct: a pattern-heavy hot tier and mixed consultation/journal/causal edges made the SCRIBE look more causal than it really was. The first safety move had to be write coordination, then rebalancing hot memory around proven scars, durable safeguards, and current multi-agent portability rules."
    validations:
      - "`scribe lock acquire/status/release` works with stale PID cleanup."
      - "Mutating commands promote, compact, archive --apply, and review-hot --apply refuse without an active SCRIBE lock."
      - "Unit tests: 40 passed after lock and typed-edge changes."
      - "`scribe stats` reports causal/evidence/consultation/journal edge counters separately."
      - "Hot tier reduced from 29 to 12 entries while preserving active SCARs, Graphify/rootless vaccines, and core retrieval/portable-bundle patterns."
    scribe_delta: "SCAR-002 | GHOST-002 | JOURNAL-045 | hot-tier rebalance"
  - id: "JOURNAL-046"
    date: "2026-05-24"
    mode: "CRITICAL"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["GHOST-002", "GHOST-003", "PAT-003", "PAT-007", "PAT-017", "PAT-020"]
    l0_abstract: "Added shared SCRIBE state synchronization for Codex IDE, Codex CLI, and other local agents."
    pourquoi: "The multi-agent failure mode was not only concurrent writes; it was stale context after another agent wrote first. A tiny state file with a real writer object, current SCRIBE hash, changed IDs, and closed write_kind enum gives each agent a cheap preflight check without pretending to be a full orchestrator."
    validations:
      - "`scribe sync` reports missing or stale state without overwriting the last writer."
      - "`scribe sync --repair` atomically writes `scribe-out/state.json` with writer object, line count, hash, last session, changed IDs, and write_kind."
      - "`scribe whoami` displays the recorded writer from state.json."
      - "Doctor emits W011 for missing state, W012 for hash mismatch, and W013 for invalid write_kind."
      - "Mutating SCRIBE commands update state.json after successful doctor validation while the lock is active."
      - "Unit tests: 44 passed after state sync coverage."
    scribe_delta: "GHOST-003 | JOURNAL-046 | state.json"
  - id: "JOURNAL-047"
    date: "2026-05-24"
    mode: "STANDARD"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["GHOST-003", "GHOST-002", "PAT-003", "PAT-017"]
    l0_abstract: "Validated the real Codex IDE versus Codex CLI stale-sync then repair scenario."
    pourquoi: "A shared state file is only useful if it detects a stale SCRIBE before work continues and if repair records the real writer without letting a reader overwrite authorship. The scenario was run on a temporary SCRIBE copy to prove the failure and recovery path without risking canonical memory."
    validations:
      - "Canonical preflight: `scribe sync --agent codex-ide --type extension` reported IN_SYNC and preserved the existing TENOR writer."
      - "Temporary codex-cli repair created a valid state file for the scenario SCRIBE copy."
      - "After a simulated codex-ide write outside state, `scribe sync --agent codex-ide --type extension` returned STALE_HASH with last_writer still codex-cli."
      - "Doctor on the stale temporary SCRIBE reported W012 state.json hash mismatch."
      - "`scribe sync --repair --agent codex-ide --type extension --write-kind repair` restored IN_SYNC and reduced doctor warnings from 7 to 6."
      - "A following codex-cli sync stayed IN_SYNC and still reported last_writer codex-ide, proving reader sync does not rewrite writer state."
      - "Canonical SCRIBE remained IN_SYNC after the scenario and doctor stayed at 0 errors."
    scribe_delta: "JOURNAL-047"
  - id: "JOURNAL-048"
    date: "2026-05-24"
    mode: "CRITICAL"
    hot_entries_consulted: ["GHOST-003", "GHOST-002", "PAT-003", "PAT-017", "PAT-020", "PAT-021"]
    l0_abstract: "Added portable bootstrap and generated-output cleanup for the SCRIBE bundle."
    pourquoi: "The bundle was safer after lock and state sync, but still relied on humans or agents to know which generated files were disposable and how to initialize a copied `.agent` in a new project. `scribe clean` codifies retention, and `scribe bootstrap` makes a copied bundle self-initializing without embedding project-specific memory in `.agent`."
    validations:
      - "`scribe clean --dry-run --graphify` identified generated reports, validation exports, dashboard screenshots, commit-plan, and Graphify AST overflow without targeting active state or graph outputs."
      - "`scribe clean --apply --graphify` removed 21 generated artifacts and left `scribe-out/` with only active dashboard, doctor report, state, and bundle graph outputs."
      - "`scribe clean --apply --agent-cache` removed `.agent` __pycache__ directories after tests."
      - "`scribe bootstrap --agent TENOR --type cli` was idempotent on the current project with unchanged AGENTS.md and .graphifyignore."
      - "Empty-project copy test: copied `.agent` to /tmp, ran `scribe bootstrap`, created SCRIBE, AGENTS.md, .graphifyignore, scribe-out/state.json, and a Graphify placeholder because no app files existed."
      - "Empty-project second bootstrap was idempotent and reported PROJET EXISTANT."
      - "Unit tests: 51 passed with Python bytecode disabled."
      - "`scribe install . --dry-run`: unchanged AGENTS.md and .graphifyignore."
      - "`scribe clean --dry-run --graphify --agent-cache`: nothing to remove after cleanup."
